# The rvasm sample directory tracks its OUTPUT and ignores its INPUTS.
#
# The distinction is the whole point of the course: a number in the prose is
# a claim about bytes, so the BYTES are committed and the things that make the
# bytes are not.  A reader can check every figure in the course against
# rvdec.out, run1.txt and run2.txt without installing a cross-compiler.  A
# reader who wants to re-derive them runs build_samples.sh and needs clang
# with a riscv64 target and llvm-objdump-21 -- and this host is the one place
# in the whole collection where even THAT is not enough, because the second
# reader is LLVM too, so the two readers share an assembler.  The artifact
# says so in section 10 rather than implying an independence it does not have.
#
# So: the recorded runs are tracked, the objects are not.  They are large,
# they are binary, and they are reproducible from two text files with one
# command.

# Object files.  All of these are outputs of build_samples.sh.  The `*.o`
# pattern is listed once as a pattern and every name is NOT listed again,
# because a name that appears in two places is a name that eventually
# disagrees with itself.
*.o

# The disassembler's own listing, for the same reason: it is derived.
*_objdump.txt

# The scratch files the measurement driver assembles.  rvdec.py writes
# _a.s/_a.o for a single-instruction probe, _b.s/_b.o for a batch, _w.s/_w.o
# for a word asked about in isolation, and _space.s/_space.o for the 49,152
# half-word enumeration of section 6 -- which is the largest file this script
# ever produces and the reason the enumeration is worth committing an OUTPUT
# for.  None of them is an input to anything; they are deleted and rewritten
# dozens of times per run.
_a.s
_a.o
_b.s
_b.o
_w.s
_w.o
_space.s
_space.o

# Python bytecode.  Present on disk because rvdec.py has been imported by
# diagnostics during development; it is never an input.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
